\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Introduction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \strokec3 What is Mo?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \strokec3 Mo is a traditional Tibetan system of divination used to seek clarity when the path ahead is uncertain. For centuries, practitioners have turned to Mo when facing questions about decisions, relationships, health, travel, obstacles, spiritual practice, and the right timing for action.\
Although Mo is often performed with dice or tokens, it is 
\f0\b \cf2 \strokec3 not
\f1\b0 \cf2 \strokec3  based on the idea that life is being handed over to chance. In the Tibetan Buddhist understanding, this is not mere randomness. Mo works through 
\f0\b \cf2 \strokec3 interdependence
\f1\b0 \cf2 \strokec3 : the meeting of mind, intention, conditions, prayer, and symbolic form.\
In this practice, the outer cast is only one part of the process. The deeper basis is the 
\f0\b \cf2 \strokec3 state of mind
\f1\b0 \cf2 \strokec3  with which the question is asked. When the mind is gathered, sincere, and clear, the result is not simply \'93what came up,\'94 but a reflection arising within a meaningful web of causes and conditions. The cast becomes a way of revealing what is already moving beneath the surface.\
This is why intention matters so much. Before consulting the Mo, one does not merely \'93ask a question.\'94 One settles the mind, holds the situation sincerely, and turns toward wisdom.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec3 Why Manjushri?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \strokec3 This form of Mo is associated with 
\f0\b \cf2 \strokec3 Manjushri
\f1\b0 \cf2 \strokec3 , the bodhisattva of wisdom. In Tibetan Buddhism, Manjushri represents clear seeing, penetrating insight, and the ability to cut through confusion.\
The mantra used in this practice is a way of orienting the mind toward that wisdom. The meaning of the word "mantra" is "that which protects the mind." We use the mantra to invoke the wisdom mind of Manjushri, gather our attention, refine our intention, and align the mind with clarity rather than fear, haste, or grasping.\
In this sense, Mo is not about trying to force an answer out of the universe. It is about 
\f0\b \cf2 \strokec3 consulting wisdom
\f1\b0 \cf2 \strokec3 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec3 A Living Tradition\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \strokec3 Mo is not a novelty system, a personality quiz, or a spiritual gimmick. It belongs to a long and respected Tibetan tradition of divinatory practice that has been relied upon for generations. It has been used not only by lay practitioners, but also by monks, teachers, and accomplished spiritual masters when clarity was needed in uncertain circumstances. Mo has been relied upon throughout the centuries for insight into undertakings, relationships, illness, travel, and spiritual questions. The system used in this app is from Jamg\'f6n Mipham\'92s Manjushri-based method and is revered as deriving its power from Manjushri and from sacred tradition. \
That lineage matters.\
Tradition does not guarantee that every question will be answered in the way we hope. But it does mean that this method has been tested, preserved, and trusted within a serious contemplative culture. Mo has endured because it is not approached as entertainment. It is approached as a ritual means of discerning conditions wisely.\
This app offers a respectful, simplified way to engage that tradition.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec3 How to Approach This Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \strokec3 The purpose of Mo is not to surrender your life to an external force. It is to receive guidance with sincerity and discernment.\
Approach the practice with:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 a calm mind\
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 a clear intention\
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 one question at a time\
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 a willingness to hear what is shown\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec3 \
The answer is not meant to replace your judgment. It is meant to illuminate it.\
Mo is strongest when approached with reverence, honesty, and openness. The more settled the mind, the more interpretable the answer tends to be.\
You are not rolling into fate.\
You are entering into a conversation with wisdom.\
}